    {
      "filename": "Photograph of Lord Mayo's party visiting Jamrud Fort in the Khyber Pass, by Baker & Burke, 1870.jpg",
      "title": "File:Photograph of Lord Mayo's party visiting Jamrud Fort in the Khyber Pass, by Baker & Burke, 1870.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Photograph_of_Lord_Mayo%27s_party_visiting_Jamrud_Fort_in_the_Khyber_Pass%2C_by_Baker_%26_Burke%2C_1870.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Lord_Mayo%27s_party_visiting_Jamrud_Fort_in_the_Khyber_Pass,_by_Baker_%26_Burke,_1870.jpg",
      "width": 960,
      "height": 444,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Baker &amp; Burke",
      "description": "Photograph of Lord Mayo's party visiting Jamrud Fort in the Khyber Pass, 1870.\nPhotograph of Lord Mayo's party taken in Jamrud Fort and one of the series of views by Baker &amp; Burke illustrating his visit to the city in 1870, whilst travelling through the North-West Frontier Province.\n\nAn image of this photograph can also be found at: [1], [2], [3], [4], [5]",
      "dateCreated": "circa 1870<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P,+1870-00-00T00:00:00Z/9,P1480,Q5727902</div>",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "jamrud-fort-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "tw4c",
      "smallPath": "_m/t/w/4/c/jamrud-fort-wp/photograph-of-lord-mayo-s-part-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Photograph of Jamrud Fort, taken by Dhanna Singh Chahal 'Patialvi', 1932.jpg",
      "title": "File:Photograph of Jamrud Fort, taken by Dhanna Singh Chahal 'Patialvi', 1932.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Photograph_of_Jamrud_Fort%2C_taken_by_Dhanna_Singh_Chahal_%27Patialvi%27%2C_1932.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_Jamrud_Fort,_taken_by_Dhanna_Singh_Chahal_%27Patialvi%27,_1932.jpg",
      "width": 1176,
      "height": 688,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Dhanna Singh Chahal 'Patialvi'",
      "description": "Photograph of Jamrud Fort, taken by Dhanna Singh Chahal 'Patialvi', 1932.\nImage source URL: [1]\nThis photograph was showcased in a webinar titled 'De-Freezing 1930's Panjab', delivered on 20 August 2011, refer to: [2]; [3]; [4]; [5]\n\n\nThe image that we see on the screen is also deeply connected to the history of Punjab. Whenever it is even briefly mentioned, we begin to speak of a very long history. When something extremely difficult or seemingly impossible is done, an idiom is used—like the Fort of Jamrud. This fort was actually built in the 18th century by Hari Singh Nalwa. In 1837, Hari Singh Nalwa was martyred at this very fort. From the time it was built until today, this fort has remained extremely important for the entire region.\nIn history, we repeatedly see that invaders would enter through the Khyber Pass and attack Hindustan, causing destruction throughout the region. This fort continued to be significant even after the end of Ranjit Singh’s rule, then under the British, and later for Pakistan as well. Today it lies in Pakistan’s former Federally Administered Tribal Area, near Peshawar. Even now, it is under the control of the Pakistan Army. For the past 60 years, since the formation of Pakistan, this fort has remained under military control, and no kind of tourist activity has been allowed there.\n\nWhen we look at this photograph, it leads our attention back to the person who took it—Bhai Dhanna Singh—and to his other photographs as well. Bhai Dhanna Singh belonged to the Patiala princely state. After visiting several gurdwaras in villages, he felt that it was necessary to preserve these gurdwaras through photography. For this purpose, he came to Patiala specifically to receive training in camera photography. The photograph of this fort was taken on 24 April 1932. Apart from this, there are no other known photographs of this fort from that time for historians or travelers.",
      "dateCreated": "24 April 1932",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "jamrud-fort-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "tw4c",
      "smallPath": "_m/t/w/4/c/jamrud-fort-wp/photograph-of-jamrud-fort-take-small.webp"
    }
  ],
